# Verification screenshots. Every one of these is rewritten by `npm run verify`
# in its package, so committing them means every green run produces a diff.
# The proof is the exit code, not the pixels.
explorer/who.png
graph/import.png
link/link.png
recovery/recovery.png
sdk/examples/hello.png
editor/shot-*.png
landing/shot-*.png
post/shot-*.png
demo/shot-*.png
prf-check/shot-*.png
wordpress/tests/card.png
bags/dispute.png

# Deployed bundles are TRACKED, deliberately.
#
# `xonly-deploy` publishes what git contains, so an ignored dist/ meant the
# deploy silently skipped both hosts and reported success. Beyond fixing that:
# these are single self-contained files served under a hash-pinned CSP and
# covered by a signer-log attestation. Tracking them means the git blob, the
# served policy and the attestation all commit to the SAME bytes, and anyone can
# check all three against each other. Building on the server instead would put
# node, npm and a dependency tree on the box that holds keys, and leave the
# served bytes reproducible from nowhere.
!signer/dist/
!signer/dist/xonly-signer.html
!signer/dist/csp.txt
!signer/dist/attestation-input.json
!editor/dist/
!editor/dist/xonly-editor.html
!editor/dist/csp.txt

# The assembled apex tree. Same reasoning as the bundles above: xonly-deploy
# publishes what git contains, and the served bytes should be reproducible from
# the repository by anyone who wants to check them.
!public/
!public/**
